1
00:00:00,720 --> 00:00:04,120
Hey everyone, we are in our chapter 13 work problems.

2
00:00:04,120 --> 00:00:07,280
I have a whole bunch of questions here that we're going to look at on price discrimination.

3
00:00:07,680 --> 00:00:10,160
Let's go ahead and take a look at the first one.

4
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:12,360
What is the main goal of price discrimination?

5
00:00:12,360 --> 00:00:19,320
Well, we know that with price discrimination the firm is attempting to take surplus from consumers in the market.

6
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:24,400
So they're trying to extract that extra value from consumers, turn it into firm profits.

7
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:26,520
So let's find an answer that looks like that.

8
00:00:26,520 --> 00:00:28,160
And C is going to be our answer there.

9
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:33,200
Convert our consumer surplus into our producer surplus.

10
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:36,800
So you're charging these consumers based on what their willingness to pay is.

11
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:43,920
This allows us to extract some, maybe all, if we're doing first degree price discrimination of the consumer surplus.

12
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:49,280
And so that actually allows us, the firm, to turn that back into producer surplus.

13
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:55,440
Question two, which of the following is an example of third degree price discrimination?

14
00:00:55,600 --> 00:00:57,480
So, um, we're gonna be looking at these.

15
00:00:57,480 --> 00:00:58,840
Charging each customer.

16
00:00:58,840 --> 00:01:00,720
No, that's first degree price discrimination.

17
00:01:01,040 --> 00:01:04,040
Charging students less than professionals for software.

18
00:01:04,040 --> 00:01:05,200
Yeah, that's our answer there.

19
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:10,120
I don't even have to go any further because we're segmenting the market based on something.  Okay.

20
00:01:10,120 --> 00:01:12,440
And that's what third degree price discrimination does.

21
00:01:12,440 --> 00:01:21,680
It segments the market based on age, occupation, location, whatever it happens to be in charges different prices based off of that segmentation.

22
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:29,760
Here we're just assuming that our students are going to be more price sensitive, that is more elastic than what our professionals are going to be.

23
00:01:30,320 --> 00:01:32,320
And that's a pretty safe assumption.

24
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:37,120
So that's why we choose B as our answer, because of segmentation in there.

25
00:01:37,920 --> 00:01:44,440
How can a firm prevent arbitrage when practicing price discrimination between markets?

26
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:52,000
So arbitrage is the thing where consumers buy at a low price and they resell at a higher price and they take the profit from that resale.

27
00:01:52,520 --> 00:01:58,280
And in order to have price discrimination actually work, you have to be able to prevent arbitrage from happening.

28
00:01:58,280 --> 00:02:00,440
So let's look at the SETI single uniform price.

29
00:02:00,680 --> 00:02:03,000
That doesn't necessarily prevent price discrimination.

30
00:02:03,240 --> 00:02:05,160
You look at any Taylor Swift concert.

31
00:02:05,320 --> 00:02:07,800
Allow product resale to increase access.

32
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:14,440
No, that doesn't do it either make it difficult or costly to resell the product between groups.  There's your answer.  C.

33
00:02:15,080 --> 00:02:16,440
We don't need to go any further.

34
00:02:16,440 --> 00:02:17,720
That is our answer here.

35
00:02:19,680 --> 00:02:29,120
What we're trying to do is basically use things like non transferable licenses in the sale of the good tie products, some kind of identity.

36
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:33,200
So you have to show your license or show your student id.

37
00:02:33,840 --> 00:02:44,240
So that would make it more difficult to resell things like that, to cause that difficulty to increase between the resale between two groups.

38
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:49,280
Robinson Patman act was designed to do what?

39
00:02:49,360 --> 00:02:52,240
And the answer here, we're gonna get an explanation here in a second.

40
00:02:52,240 --> 00:02:56,280
But the answer is prevent price discrimination that harms small businesses.

41
00:02:56,280 --> 00:03:11,280
So the Robinson Patent act came out in 1936, targeted anti competitive price discrimination, really looking at larger firms selling at discounts to other larger firms and harming businesses, smaller businesses in the process.

42
00:03:11,680 --> 00:03:15,240
So your large sellers would sell to your large buyers.

43
00:03:15,240 --> 00:03:17,200
The small buyers, like, hey, what about me?

44
00:03:17,360 --> 00:03:19,520
Well, that's what the Robinson Patman act came in.

45
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:23,040
All right, so that's what it was designed to do.

46
00:03:24,240 --> 00:03:30,800
Which pricing strategy is most likely to violate the Robinson Patman Act?

47
00:03:31,120 --> 00:03:43,120
So again, Robinson Patman act was looking at specifically targeting these large sellers from selling to these large buyers at discounts that the small buyers, buyers couldn't get.

48
00:03:43,760 --> 00:03:46,320
So let's look at these.

49
00:03:46,640 --> 00:03:48,720
Offering a temporary promotion.  Never stop.

50
00:03:49,040 --> 00:03:51,040
No, Everybody looks like they might get that.

51
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:53,960
Selling the same product to large and small buyers at different prices.

52
00:03:53,960 --> 00:03:56,000
Yeah, that's your, that's your answer.

53
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:58,960
And the cost justification part is really key there.

54
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:10,440
Without cost justification, if that large buyer can have some type of cost justification to, or large seller has some kind of cost justification to the large buyer, then it's okay.

55
00:04:10,440 --> 00:04:13,760
But they don't have that cost justification in there, then it's not okay.

56
00:04:13,760 --> 00:04:17,840
It's a violation of the Robinson Patent Act.

57
00:04:17,840 --> 00:04:20,760
So we do allow these discounts tied to volume.

58
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:30,440
So if your large buyers buying in such bulk that they can get that cost change, then it's okay, but otherwise it's not okay.

59
00:04:31,880 --> 00:04:36,720
Which of the following is an example of first degree price discrimination?

60
00:04:36,720 --> 00:04:42,360
This is also called the perfect price discrimination because we're charging each consumer their willingness to pay.

61
00:04:43,320 --> 00:04:45,480
Let's look at what our answers are.

62
00:04:45,720 --> 00:04:47,320
Offering senior discounts.

63
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:53,200
That's not, that's more of a segmentation or third degree price discrimination charging different prices by country.

64
00:04:53,200 --> 00:05:00,840
Again, third degree price discrimination, you're looking at location as a way to segment markets and a car dealer negotiating different prices with each buyer.

65
00:05:00,840 --> 00:05:02,080
Yeah, that's your answer right there.

66
00:05:02,080 --> 00:05:07,840
You're figuring out what each buyer's willingness to pay is and then you're charging them their willingness to pay.

67
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:17,840
So you're extracting all the consumer surplus for or most, maybe not all, but most of the consumer surplus out of that market and turning it into producer surplus.

68
00:05:18,720 --> 00:05:19,840
All right, next question.

69
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:25,840
Which of the following is best classified as second degree price discrimination?

70
00:05:25,840 --> 00:05:27,680
So we talk a little bit about this.

71
00:05:27,680 --> 00:05:33,680
I believe in this chapter we don't talk much about it because second degree price discrimination is more indirect price discrimination.

72
00:05:33,680 --> 00:05:45,040
It's where you look at things like metering quantity sold, volume sold, self selection into particular, particular goods being purchased.

73
00:05:45,440 --> 00:05:48,440
So we're going to keep those things in mind while going down these answers.

74
00:05:48,440 --> 00:05:52,640
Offering subscription discounts to students, that's not going to be our answer.

75
00:05:52,640 --> 00:05:53,840
That's going to be third degree.

76
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:56,240
Charging higher prices for shipping overseas.

77
00:05:56,240 --> 00:05:57,360
That's going to be third degree.

78
00:05:57,880 --> 00:06:01,080
Offering lower per unit prices for bulk purchases.

79
00:06:01,240 --> 00:06:05,560
That is going to be second degree price discrimination.

80
00:06:06,280 --> 00:06:15,760
Here you're going to allow self selection take place because people who won't get in those bulk purchases will self select into making those bulk purchases.

81
00:06:15,760 --> 00:06:19,000
So that's why this is second degree price discrimination.

82
00:06:20,040 --> 00:06:22,040
And our last question here.

83
00:06:22,600 --> 00:06:26,400
Movie theater has two customer groups, students and adults.

84
00:06:26,400 --> 00:06:29,800
Students are willing to pay $8, adults willing to pay $12.

85
00:06:30,280 --> 00:06:32,680
Marginal cost of each ticket, zero.  How nice.

86
00:06:32,840 --> 00:06:36,120
And then we have 100 students and 100 adults who plan to attend.

87
00:06:36,120 --> 00:06:42,320
And our scenario says we can charge 8 to everyone or 8 to students and 12 to adults.

88
00:06:42,320 --> 00:06:48,520
So we are trying to practice third degree price discrimination in that second part of the scenario there.

89
00:06:49,160 --> 00:06:52,680
So it says how much additional revenue does the theater earn from price discrimination?

90
00:06:52,680 --> 00:06:54,800
And this is why firms want to price discriminate.

91
00:06:54,800 --> 00:06:56,280
Let's look at the actual numbers here.

92
00:06:56,720 --> 00:06:58,640
So we could charge eight to everyone.

93
00:06:58,800 --> 00:07:00,320
We have 200 total people.

94
00:07:00,880 --> 00:07:04,160
So 200 times eight gives us $1600.

95
00:07:05,120 --> 00:07:12,640
Or we could charge eight to students and 12 to adults.

96
00:07:12,640 --> 00:07:14,160
And this is what theaters do.

97
00:07:15,040 --> 00:07:18,160
We get 800 plus 1200.

98
00:07:18,800 --> 00:07:23,360
And that gives us a total amount of revenue of $2,000.

99
00:07:23,440 --> 00:07:27,120
The difference between those is $400.

100
00:07:27,120 --> 00:07:35,440
And that is how you take price discrimination, put it in action and you take consumer surplus out of the market and put it into your firm's profits.

101
00:07:35,680 --> 00:07:40,200
All right, that is it for our Chapter 13 questions.

102
00:07:40,200 --> 00:07:41,200
Hope you've enjoyed it.